        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "54ed2a9c-8251-5926-9769-5a44b9d12c92",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: William T. Anderson, Jesse James, William Quantrill\n- Events: Centralia Massacre\n- Places: Centralia, Missouri\n- Topics: American Civil War, pro-Confederate guerrillas\n\nFacts:\n- On September 27, 1864, the Centralia Massacre occurred at Centralia, Missouri during the American Civil War.\n- The Centralia Massacre involved the capture and execution of twenty-four unarmed Union soldiers.\n- William T. Anderson led the pro-Confederate guerrillas responsible for the Centralia Massacre.\n- William T. Anderson, also known as \"Bloody Bill\" Anderson, lived from 1840 to October 26, 1864.\n- William T. Anderson was one of the deadliest and most brutal pro-Confederate guerrilla leaders in the American Civil War.\n- Jesse James was a follower of William Quantrill and \"Bloody Bill\" Anderson.\n- Jesse James and other followers were accused of participating in atrocities against Union soldiers and civilian abolitionists, including the Centralia Massacre in 1864.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "7c9cd553-7bb4-5f37-9362-0c3d21c853f8",
        "text": "Page: Centralia Massacre (Missouri). Sentence: The Centralia Massacre was an incident during the American Civil War in which twenty-four unarmed Union soldiers were captured and executed at Centralia, Missouri on September 27, 1864 by the pro-Confederate guerrilla leader William T. Anderson.\nPage: William T. Anderson. Sentence: William T. Anderson (1840 – October 26, 1864)—known as \"Bloody Bill\" Anderson—was one of the deadliest and most brutal pro-Confederate guerrilla leaders in the American Civil War.\nPage: Jesse James. Sentence: As followers of William Quantrill and \"Bloody Bill\" Anderson, they were accused of participating in atrocities against Union soldiers and civilian abolitionists, including the Centralia Massacre in 1864.",
